 ## Analyses
 
-- **G4Hunter on telomeric motifs.** Telomeric repeat motifs from 49 eukaryotic species (19 distinct motifs; `species.tsv`) are tiled to 600 nt and scored with G4Hunter (Bedrat et al., NAR 2016).
-- **G4Hunter on the hTERT proximal promoter.** A 400-nt window (chr5:1,294,991-1,295,390, GRCh38; -2 to -401 relative to the TERT ATG) is scored on the G-rich template (plus) strand, for the wild type and the C228T (-124C>T) and C250T (-146C>T) hotspot mutations, modelled as G>A on the plus strand.
-- **DNA language-model embeddings.** Each distinct motif is tiled to 1,200 nt and embedded with [Nucleotide Transformer v2](https://huggingface.co/InstaDeepAI/nucleotide-transformer-v2-500m-multi-species) (500M, multi-species; attention-masked mean pooling of the final layer). Species that share a motif share an embedding, so only the 19 distinct motifs are embedded. Similarity is shown as a cosine-similarity heatmap with average-linkage clustering.
-- **Physicochemical descriptors.** Structures of 17 small molecules are retrieved from PubChem by name (CIDs recorded in `results/compounds_resolved.tsv`), reduced to the parent structure (largest fragment), and described with RDKit.
+- **DNA language-model similarity** (Figure 1, Supplementary Figure 1; Supplementary Methods S1). The 49-species panel (`species.tsv`, Supplementary Table S1) contains 19 distinct telomeric motifs. Each motif is tiled to 1,200 nt and embedded with [Nucleotide Transformer v2](https://huggingface.co/InstaDeepAI/nucleotide-transformer-v2-500m-multi-species) (500M, multi-species) by attention-masked mean pooling of the final layer. Species that share a motif share an embedding, so only the 19 distinct motifs are embedded. Figure 1 shows pairwise cosine similarity with average-linkage clustering (optimal leaf ordering); Supplementary Figure 1 is a UMAP of the same embeddings (cosine metric, 5 neighbours, minimum distance 0.5, seed 42).
+- **G4Hunter on telomeric motifs** (Figure 3; Supplementary Methods S2). Each motif is tiled to 600 nt and scored on the G-rich strand with G4Hunter (Bedrat et al., 2016). Supplementary Table S2 of the paper compares these predictions with published experimental evidence.
+- **G4Hunter on the hTERT proximal promoter** (Figures 5 and 7; Supplementary Methods S3). A 400-nt window (GRCh38 chr5:1,294,991-1,295,390, 1-based; -2 to -401 relative to the TERT ATG) is scored with 25-nt sliding windows on the plus strand, which is the template strand of TERT and is G-rich in this region. The C228T (-124C>T) and C250T (-146C>T) hotspot mutations are modelled as G>A on the plus strand, singly and together. Figure 7 enlarges -181 to -89.
+- **Physicochemical descriptors** (Supplementary Figure 2; Supplementary Methods S5). Structures of 17 small molecules are retrieved from PubChem by name (CIDs recorded), reduced to the parent structure (largest fragment) and described with RDKit: molecular weight, Crippen logP, TPSA, hydrogen-bond donors and acceptors, rotatable bonds and aromatic rings.
 
