 ## Limitations
 
-- The embeddings reflect the sequence of the repeat unit only; they carry no information on phylogeny, repeat heterogeneity, telomere length or chromatin.
-- G4Hunter is a sequence-based propensity score; the |score| > 1.0 cut-off is heuristic, and the mean score of a strictly periodic tract is fixed by the composition of its repeat unit.
-- The hTERT analysis predicts local changes in G-richness only. The established mechanism by which C228T and C250T activate TERT is the creation of ETS/GABP binding sites (Bell et al., Science 2015).
-- Descriptors summarise general drug-likeness and do not predict G4 binding or activity.
+- The embeddings reflect the sequence of the repeat unit only; they carry no information on phylogeny, repeat heterogeneity within an array, telomere length or chromatin. Similarity in this representation is not evidence of common ancestry or convergent evolution.
+- G4Hunter is a sequence-based propensity score. The |score| > 1.0 threshold is a heuristic, and the mean score of a tiled repeat is fixed by the composition of the repeat unit. Repeats with only GG tracts (insects, nematodes) score below 1.0, yet fold into two-tetrad G-quadruplexes in vitro.
+- The predicted reduction in G4 propensity at the hTERT hotspots is a local sequence effect and does not test G4 destabilisation in cells. The established mechanism of hTERT activation by these mutations is the creation of ETS binding sites recognised by GABP (Bell et al., 2015).
+- The physicochemical descriptors summarise general drug-likeness only and do not predict G4 binding or activity. Calculated logP is not meaningful for the permanently charged cations berberine, RHPS4 and TMPyP4.
+- Motifs whose source is TeloBase in `species.tsv` should be checked against [TeloBase](https://cfb.ceitec.muni.cz/telobase/) before reuse; several fungal motifs are reported differently across sources.
 
